{"page_content": "ACME/Flame Design Final Report (2023) \n \n6 April 2023 \n49 \n \n \nthe detection, the hot-wire igniter was deactivated and retracted, which was of course \nan issue when the flame has yet to ignite.  This was typically avoided by detecting \nignition with measurement of the flame\u2019s OH* chemiluminescence with a \nPhotomultiplier Tube (PMT) module.  The OH* chemiluminescence, in the ultraviolet \nat 310 nm, is a good indicator of an ignited flame rather than the glowing igniter, \nwhich emits primarily in the infrared and visible spectrum.  However, the combination \nof igniter settings and PMT gains can lead to false ignition detection. \n \nI. Completeness/Quality of Data \nThe 326 flames ignited in the two rounds of greatly exceeds the 76 tests which were \nspecified as required in the ACME Integrated Science Requirements Document (ISRD).  As \na result, more than 4.2 times as many flames were ignited than required. \n \nHowever, as discussed in Section III.A, the performance of the first spherical burner was \nnot always satisfactory \u2013 where the flame was not always sufficiently spherical or \ncentered on the burner, suggesting extreme asymmetry in the flow field and thus \nquestionable data.  Yet, 240 flames were ignited with the second burner, which is over \n3.1 times the number of required tests. \n \nACME\u2019s sintered spherical burners do not yield spherically symmetric flames which are \ncentered on the burner\u2019s center, where that combination is important for true spherical \nsymmetry.  This lack of symmetry was especially evident at high burner flows.  It is very \nlikely that the sintered attachment of the sphere to the supply tube is more porous than \nthe sintered sphere itself because the flame is seen to be shifted toward the supply tube.  \nThere was significant improvement when the starting burner (serial number 1002) was \nreplaced mid-way through round 1 with an alternate burner (serial number 1003).  But \nthe symmetry is not perfect and this is a shortcoming of the Flame Design data. \n \nPortions of ACME\u2019s recorded image data were sometimes deleted without downlinking.  \nThis was done because of operational constraints which limit the amount of data that can \nbe managed per test day.  Given the constraint, the decision was sometimes made to \nsacrifice some image data (e.g., from the HiBMs camera) to allow additional tests to be \nconducted.  This was intentionally done in order to maximize the scientific return of each \nday of testing within the project constraints.", "metadata": {"source": "PSI-10_Reports_FinalRpt_ACMEFlameDesign.pdf", "page": 49}}